1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,368
Do you remember your very first paycheck?

2
00:00:02,548 --> 00:00:03,565
Not the amount,

3
00:00:03,565 --> 00:00:05,125
maybe, but the feeling.

4
00:00:05,305 --> 00:00:07,609
The envelope with your name typed on

5
00:00:07,609 --> 00:00:07,801
it.

6
00:00:07,981 --> 00:00:09,519
The way you held it a little

7
00:00:09,519 --> 00:00:11,496
longer than you needed to before you

8
00:00:11,496 --> 00:00:12,045
opened it.

9
00:00:12,225 --> 00:00:13,968
For millions of Americans,

10
00:00:13,968 --> 00:00:16,650
that moment came in nineteen fifty-nine,

11
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:18,795
and it felt like the whole world

12
00:00:18,795 --> 00:00:20,538
had just opened its doors.

13
00:00:20,887 --> 00:00:22,755
In nineteen fifty-nine,

14
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:25,353
a young person could walk into a

15
00:00:25,353 --> 00:00:26,895
factory, an office,

16
00:00:26,895 --> 00:00:28,438
a store or a diner,

17
00:00:28,438 --> 00:00:30,630
and walk out with a job the

18
00:00:30,630 --> 00:00:31,847
same afternoon.

19
00:00:32,027 --> 00:00:34,175
The country was growing fast.

20
00:00:34,355 --> 00:00:36,707
New highways, new suburbs,

21
00:00:36,707 --> 00:00:38,244
new supermarkets.

22
00:00:38,424 --> 00:00:40,892
Everything needed people to build it,

23
00:00:40,892 --> 00:00:42,759
run it, sell it and sweep up

24
00:00:42,759 --> 00:00:43,359
after it.

25
00:00:43,708 --> 00:00:45,392
So let's go back.

26
00:00:45,572 --> 00:00:47,625
To the first job you ever had,

27
00:00:47,625 --> 00:00:49,405
the boss you never forgot,

28
00:00:49,405 --> 00:00:51,322
the uniform you had to wear,

29
00:00:51,322 --> 00:00:53,649
and the things that job taught you

30
00:00:53,649 --> 00:00:55,429
that no school ever could.

31
00:00:55,609 --> 00:00:57,573
These are first job memories,

32
00:00:57,573 --> 00:00:59,266
from nineteen fifty-nine.

33
00:00:59,615 --> 00:01:00,739
For a lot of us,

34
00:01:00,739 --> 00:01:03,479
work started long before the first real

35
00:01:03,479 --> 00:01:03,760
job.

36
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,327
A paper route at eleven or twelve,

37
00:01:06,327 --> 00:01:08,853
with a canvas bag over your shoulder

38
00:01:08,853 --> 00:01:11,661
and fifty houses to reach before school.

39
00:01:11,841 --> 00:01:14,147
You folded every newspaper by hand,

40
00:01:14,147 --> 00:01:16,321
threw it onto the porch from your

41
00:01:16,321 --> 00:01:19,089
bicycle, and on Friday afternoons you went

42
00:01:19,089 --> 00:01:21,263
door to door to collect what each

43
00:01:21,263 --> 00:01:22,185
customer owed.

44
00:01:22,535 --> 00:01:24,535
You learned quickly who tipped and who

45
00:01:24,535 --> 00:01:24,904
didn't.

46
00:01:25,084 --> 00:01:26,406
Who paid right away,

47
00:01:26,406 --> 00:01:28,654
and who always said come back next

48
00:01:28,654 --> 00:01:28,985
week.

49
00:01:29,165 --> 00:01:32,260
That paper route taught a twelve-year-old more

50
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:35,086
about money and people than any arithmetic

51
00:01:35,086 --> 00:01:35,423
book.

52
00:01:35,603 --> 00:01:36,815
And at Christmas,

53
00:01:36,815 --> 00:01:39,669
the good customers left an envelope with

54
00:01:39,669 --> 00:01:40,881
an extra quarter,

55
00:01:40,881 --> 00:01:42,522
or even a whole dollar.

56
00:01:42,872 --> 00:01:45,064
Girls babysat for the neighbors,

57
00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:47,735
for thirty-five or fifty cents an hour,

58
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:50,886
and raided the refrigerator after the children

59
00:01:50,886 --> 00:01:51,708
were asleep.

60
00:01:51,888 --> 00:01:54,637
Boys mowed lawns with push mowers that

61
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:56,156
had no engine at all,

62
00:01:56,156 --> 00:01:58,905
and shoveled snow off driveways in the

63
00:01:58,905 --> 00:01:59,411
winter.

64
00:01:59,591 --> 00:02:01,892
Every dollar went into a jar,

65
00:02:01,892 --> 00:02:04,749
or a passbook savings account at the

66
00:02:04,749 --> 00:02:05,860
bank downtown.

67
00:02:06,210 --> 00:02:07,353
And in the summer,

68
00:02:07,353 --> 00:02:09,194
there were the seasonal jobs.

69
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:12,708
Picking strawberries or detasseling corn in the

70
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:13,275
Midwest.

71
00:02:13,455 --> 00:02:16,036
Working the snack bar at the public

72
00:02:16,036 --> 00:02:16,404
pool.

73
00:02:16,584 --> 00:02:19,124
Bagging groceries at the supermarket,

74
00:02:19,124 --> 00:02:21,527
where the manager taught you to put

75
00:02:21,527 --> 00:02:23,586
the cans on the bottom and the

76
00:02:23,586 --> 00:02:24,479
bread on top,

77
00:02:24,479 --> 00:02:26,401
and to always say thank you,

78
00:02:26,401 --> 00:02:26,813
ma'am.

79
00:02:27,162 --> 00:02:29,816
Getting a real job in nineteen fifty-nine

80
00:02:29,816 --> 00:02:31,563
was simpler than it is now.

81
00:02:31,743 --> 00:02:34,080
You read the help wanted ads in

82
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:37,248
the newspaper, which were divided into two

83
00:02:37,248 --> 00:02:39,661
columns, one for men and one for

84
00:02:39,661 --> 00:02:40,113
women.

85
00:02:40,293 --> 00:02:42,913
Or you heard about an opening from

86
00:02:42,913 --> 00:02:44,531
an uncle, a neighbor,

87
00:02:44,531 --> 00:02:46,226
or a friend at church.

88
00:02:46,406 --> 00:02:48,187
Then you put on your best clothes

89
00:02:48,187 --> 00:02:49,483
and went down in person.

90
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:52,350
There was no computer and usually no

91
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:52,840
resume.

92
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:55,944
You filled out a one-page application in

93
00:02:55,944 --> 00:02:57,918
pen at a little desk in the

94
00:02:57,918 --> 00:02:59,161
personnel office.

95
00:02:59,341 --> 00:03:01,335
Then you sat across from a man

96
00:03:01,335 --> 00:03:01,999
in a suit,

97
00:03:01,999 --> 00:03:04,325
who asked where you went to school,

98
00:03:04,325 --> 00:03:05,721
what your father did,

99
00:03:05,721 --> 00:03:08,246
and whether you could start on Monday.

100
00:03:08,426 --> 00:03:10,818
Often, that was the whole interview.

101
00:03:11,168 --> 00:03:12,607
A handshake mattered.

102
00:03:12,787 --> 00:03:14,772
Looking a man in the eye mattered.

103
00:03:14,952 --> 00:03:16,149
Showing up on time,

104
00:03:16,149 --> 00:03:18,417
with your shoes shined and your hair

105
00:03:18,417 --> 00:03:21,314
combed, mattered more than almost anything you

106
00:03:21,314 --> 00:03:22,952
could write down on paper.

107
00:03:23,132 --> 00:03:25,323
Your father had told you so the

108
00:03:25,323 --> 00:03:27,232
night before, and for once,

109
00:03:27,232 --> 00:03:28,716
he was exactly right.

110
00:03:29,067 --> 00:03:32,332
Some jobs required a physical examination first,

111
00:03:32,332 --> 00:03:34,509
and some asked for a letter from

112
00:03:34,509 --> 00:03:37,435
your minister or your high school principal

113
00:03:37,435 --> 00:03:39,748
saying you were of good character.

114
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:43,067
Girls applying for office work often took

115
00:03:43,067 --> 00:03:45,057
a typing test on the spot,

116
00:03:45,057 --> 00:03:46,206
in a back room,

117
00:03:46,206 --> 00:03:49,345
with a stopwatch ticking and a supervisor

118
00:03:49,345 --> 00:03:51,259
watching every keystroke.

119
00:03:51,609 --> 00:03:53,977
The first day was always the hardest.

120
00:03:54,157 --> 00:03:56,787
You didn't know where the lunchroom was,

121
00:03:56,787 --> 00:03:57,839
or the restroom,

122
00:03:57,839 --> 00:04:00,009
or which of the older workers you

123
00:04:00,009 --> 00:04:00,798
could trust.

124
00:04:00,978 --> 00:04:03,524
Somebody always played a joke on the

125
00:04:03,524 --> 00:04:04,089
new kid.

126
00:04:04,269 --> 00:04:06,660
Go ask the foreman for a left-handed

127
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:07,125
wrench.

128
00:04:07,305 --> 00:04:09,140
Go find a bucket of steam.

129
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,131
Everybody fell for it once.

130
00:04:11,481 --> 00:04:13,045
If you worked in a factory,

131
00:04:13,045 --> 00:04:14,725
you got a time card with your

132
00:04:14,725 --> 00:04:15,362
name on it,

133
00:04:15,362 --> 00:04:17,390
and you learned to punch the clock.

134
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:18,592
In at seven.

135
00:04:18,772 --> 00:04:20,397
Lunch at eleven-thirty.

136
00:04:20,577 --> 00:04:21,935
Out at three-thirty.

137
00:04:22,115 --> 00:04:24,243
Be one minute late and the card

138
00:04:24,243 --> 00:04:25,684
showed it in red ink,

139
00:04:25,684 --> 00:04:27,879
and the foreman showed it on his

140
00:04:27,879 --> 00:04:28,222
face.

141
00:04:28,573 --> 00:04:30,645
If you worked in an office,

142
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:31,797
you got a desk,

143
00:04:31,797 --> 00:04:34,407
a typewriter or an adding machine,

144
00:04:34,407 --> 00:04:36,480
and a stack of work by nine

145
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:37,094
o'clock.

146
00:04:37,274 --> 00:04:39,805
The men wore white shirts and ties

147
00:04:39,805 --> 00:04:41,071
every single day.

148
00:04:41,251 --> 00:04:42,958
The women wore dresses,

149
00:04:42,958 --> 00:04:44,443
stockings and heels.

150
00:04:44,623 --> 00:04:46,533
And everybody, it seemed,

151
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:48,213
smoked at their desks,

152
00:04:48,213 --> 00:04:50,734
so the whole office lived under a

153
00:04:50,734 --> 00:04:51,880
soft gray haze.

154
00:04:52,230 --> 00:04:54,018
If you worked in a store,

155
00:04:54,018 --> 00:04:56,093
you wore a uniform or a smock

156
00:04:56,093 --> 00:04:57,953
with your name sewn on it.

157
00:04:58,133 --> 00:05:00,085
You learned to make change in your

158
00:05:00,085 --> 00:05:02,497
head, counting it back into the customer's

159
00:05:02,497 --> 00:05:04,851
hand, starting from the price and working

160
00:05:04,851 --> 00:05:06,516
up to the bill they gave you.

161
00:05:06,696 --> 00:05:09,157
The cash register rang with a bell.

162
00:05:09,337 --> 00:05:11,367
There was no screen telling you what

163
00:05:11,367 --> 00:05:12,100
to give back.

164
00:05:12,451 --> 00:05:14,354
For a lot of young people,

165
00:05:14,354 --> 00:05:16,331
that first job was at a gas

166
00:05:16,331 --> 00:05:17,575
station, a diner,

167
00:05:17,575 --> 00:05:19,406
or a drive-in restaurant,

168
00:05:19,406 --> 00:05:22,188
where the carhops brought trays out to

169
00:05:22,188 --> 00:05:24,604
the cars and hooked them onto the

170
00:05:24,604 --> 00:05:25,848
half-open window.

171
00:05:26,028 --> 00:05:27,292
You ran all evening,

172
00:05:27,292 --> 00:05:29,693
smelled like french fries when you got

173
00:05:29,693 --> 00:05:32,031
home, and wouldn't have traded it for

174
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:32,600
anything.

175
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:34,724
And if you worked on a farm,

176
00:05:34,724 --> 00:05:36,816
which was still the first job for

177
00:05:36,816 --> 00:05:38,780
millions of rural young people,

178
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:40,682
there was no first day at all.

179
00:05:40,862 --> 00:05:42,762
You had been working since you were

180
00:05:42,762 --> 00:05:44,391
tall enough to carry a bucket.

181
00:05:44,571 --> 00:05:47,046
The difference now was that a neighbor

182
00:05:47,046 --> 00:05:49,130
paid you real wages to bale hay,

183
00:05:49,130 --> 00:05:51,540
milk cows, or drive a tractor through

184
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:52,907
the long summer days.

185
00:05:53,258 --> 00:05:55,005
In nineteen fifty-nine,

186
00:05:55,005 --> 00:05:57,967
the federal minimum wage was one dollar

187
00:05:57,967 --> 00:05:58,575
an hour.

188
00:05:58,755 --> 00:06:01,257
A factory job with a union might

189
00:06:01,257 --> 00:06:03,759
pay two dollars an hour or more.

190
00:06:03,939 --> 00:06:07,051
A young secretary might earn around fifty

191
00:06:07,051 --> 00:06:08,873
or sixty dollars a week.

192
00:06:09,053 --> 00:06:11,472
And for a teenager still living at

193
00:06:11,472 --> 00:06:14,460
home, even the smallest paycheck felt like

194
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:15,172
a fortune.

195
00:06:15,522 --> 00:06:18,285
Many companies still paid in cash,

196
00:06:18,285 --> 00:06:20,398
in a small brown envelope,

197
00:06:20,398 --> 00:06:21,454
every Friday.

198
00:06:21,634 --> 00:06:23,642
Your name and the amount were written

199
00:06:23,642 --> 00:06:24,455
on the outside.

200
00:06:24,635 --> 00:06:26,879
Inside were the bills and coins,

201
00:06:26,879 --> 00:06:29,474
counted out by someone in the payroll

202
00:06:29,474 --> 00:06:29,964
office.

203
00:06:30,144 --> 00:06:31,226
You signed for it,

204
00:06:31,226 --> 00:06:32,908
slipped it into your pocket,

205
00:06:32,908 --> 00:06:34,651
and felt it there all the way

206
00:06:34,651 --> 00:06:34,951
home.

207
00:06:35,301 --> 00:06:37,054
And in a great many families,

208
00:06:37,054 --> 00:06:38,988
the first thing you did was give

209
00:06:38,988 --> 00:06:40,560
part of it to your mother.

210
00:06:40,740 --> 00:06:41,941
Room and board,

211
00:06:41,941 --> 00:06:43,143
they called it.

212
00:06:43,323 --> 00:06:44,607
Ten dollars a week,

213
00:06:44,607 --> 00:06:46,702
or whatever your parents asked.

214
00:06:46,882 --> 00:06:48,820
Nobody thought of it as unfair.

215
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,822
You lived under their roof,

216
00:06:50,822 --> 00:06:52,104
you ate their food,

217
00:06:52,104 --> 00:06:53,791
and now you were helping,

218
00:06:53,791 --> 00:06:56,086
the way they had once helped their

219
00:06:56,086 --> 00:06:56,895
own parents.

220
00:06:57,246 --> 00:06:58,455
With what was left,

221
00:06:58,455 --> 00:07:01,065
you bought things that felt like freedom.

222
00:07:01,245 --> 00:07:03,022
A transistor radio.

223
00:07:03,202 --> 00:07:05,129
A new pair of shoes.

224
00:07:05,309 --> 00:07:07,799
A record album for about three or

225
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:08,780
four dollars.

226
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:10,608
Saved up for a few months,

227
00:07:10,608 --> 00:07:12,826
it might become the down payment on

228
00:07:12,826 --> 00:07:13,524
a used car,

229
00:07:13,524 --> 00:07:15,996
which was the most important purchase a

230
00:07:15,996 --> 00:07:17,517
young person could make.

231
00:07:17,867 --> 00:07:19,804
A lot of young people also opened

232
00:07:19,804 --> 00:07:22,093
their first checking account that year,

233
00:07:22,093 --> 00:07:24,030
with a little book of checks that

234
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:26,202
had their name printed in the corner.

235
00:07:26,382 --> 00:07:29,001
You wrote out your first check carefully,

236
00:07:29,001 --> 00:07:30,597
in your best handwriting,

237
00:07:30,597 --> 00:07:32,704
and then you worried about it for

238
00:07:32,704 --> 00:07:34,620
a week, until you were sure it

239
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:35,578
hadn't bounced.

240
00:07:35,928 --> 00:07:38,357
Taxes came out of your pay too,

241
00:07:38,357 --> 00:07:40,943
which surprised almost everybody.

242
00:07:41,123 --> 00:07:44,571
Federal income tax and Social Security.

243
00:07:44,751 --> 00:07:46,900
You stared at the stub and wondered

244
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:48,251
who on earth FICA was,

245
00:07:48,251 --> 00:07:50,278
and why he was taking your money.

246
00:07:50,458 --> 00:07:52,771
Your father laughed and told you welcome

247
00:07:52,771 --> 00:07:53,465
to the club.

248
00:07:53,815 --> 00:07:55,765
Almost everybody had one.

249
00:07:55,945 --> 00:07:58,012
The boss who was tough but fair.

250
00:07:58,192 --> 00:08:00,457
Who yelled when you made a mistake,

251
00:08:00,457 --> 00:08:01,946
and then, a week later,

252
00:08:01,946 --> 00:08:04,212
quietly told the owner you were the

253
00:08:04,212 --> 00:08:06,284
best worker he'd hired in years.

254
00:08:06,464 --> 00:08:08,031
You may not have liked him then.

255
00:08:08,211 --> 00:08:10,711
But you probably still think about him

256
00:08:10,711 --> 00:08:12,948
now, more often than you'd expect.

257
00:08:13,298 --> 00:08:15,236
Or the older woman in the office

258
00:08:15,236 --> 00:08:16,931
who took you under her wing.

259
00:08:17,111 --> 00:08:18,769
Who showed you how to file,

260
00:08:18,769 --> 00:08:20,243
how to answer the phone,

261
00:08:20,243 --> 00:08:22,698
and how to handle the difficult customer

262
00:08:22,698 --> 00:08:24,356
without losing your temper.

263
00:08:24,536 --> 00:08:26,498
She'd been there twenty years.

264
00:08:26,678 --> 00:08:28,650
She knew where everything was,

265
00:08:28,650 --> 00:08:30,557
and everybody, even the boss,

266
00:08:30,557 --> 00:08:32,332
was a little afraid of her.

267
00:08:32,682 --> 00:08:35,488
Those people taught you things nobody writes

268
00:08:35,488 --> 00:08:36,316
down anymore.

269
00:08:36,496 --> 00:08:37,623
Show up early.

270
00:08:37,803 --> 00:08:39,115
Finish what you start.

271
00:08:39,295 --> 00:08:42,371
Take pride in work nobody will notice.

272
00:08:42,551 --> 00:08:45,129
Don't complain in front of the customers.

273
00:08:45,309 --> 00:08:47,927
Lessons like that stayed with you for

274
00:08:47,927 --> 00:08:50,759
fifty years, through every job that came

275
00:08:50,759 --> 00:08:51,183
after.

276
00:08:51,534 --> 00:08:53,017
And then there were the friends you

277
00:08:53,017 --> 00:08:53,229
made.

278
00:08:53,409 --> 00:08:55,525
The other new hires who started the

279
00:08:55,525 --> 00:08:56,613
same week you did.

280
00:08:56,793 --> 00:08:58,381
You ate lunch together,

281
00:08:58,381 --> 00:08:59,969
covered for each other,

282
00:08:59,969 --> 00:09:02,179
went bowling on Thursday nights,

283
00:09:02,179 --> 00:09:04,734
and stood up at each other's weddings

284
00:09:04,734 --> 00:09:05,976
a few years later.

285
00:09:06,156 --> 00:09:08,492
Some of those friendships lasted the rest

286
00:09:08,492 --> 00:09:09,233
of your life.

287
00:09:09,582 --> 00:09:12,526
Here's what may surprise younger people the

288
00:09:12,526 --> 00:09:12,868
most.

289
00:09:13,048 --> 00:09:14,781
In nineteen fifty-nine,

290
00:09:14,781 --> 00:09:16,739
a lot of people who took a

291
00:09:16,739 --> 00:09:19,526
first job at eighteen stayed with the

292
00:09:19,526 --> 00:09:22,464
same company for thirty or forty years.

293
00:09:22,644 --> 00:09:24,589
The company was loyal to them,

294
00:09:24,589 --> 00:09:26,858
and they were loyal to the company.

295
00:09:27,038 --> 00:09:28,351
When they retired,

296
00:09:28,351 --> 00:09:29,738
there was a dinner,

297
00:09:29,738 --> 00:09:31,999
a speech, and very often a gold

298
00:09:31,999 --> 00:09:32,437
watch.

299
00:09:32,786 --> 00:09:35,651
There were company picnics in the summer,

300
00:09:35,651 --> 00:09:38,655
with three-legged races and a softball game

301
00:09:38,655 --> 00:09:40,960
between the office and the plant.

302
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:43,015
There was a Christmas party,

303
00:09:43,015 --> 00:09:44,689
and a turkey or a ham for

304
00:09:44,689 --> 00:09:46,631
every family at Thanksgiving.

305
00:09:46,811 --> 00:09:49,629
The company newsletter printed your wedding,

306
00:09:49,629 --> 00:09:51,358
the birth of your children,

307
00:09:51,358 --> 00:09:52,895
and your bowling scores.

308
00:09:53,244 --> 00:09:55,500
For the young men who were drafted,

309
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:57,691
a first job often waited for them.

310
00:09:57,871 --> 00:09:59,961
The law protected their place,

311
00:09:59,961 --> 00:10:02,469
and many employers held the job open

312
00:10:02,469 --> 00:10:04,698
for two years while they served.

313
00:10:04,878 --> 00:10:07,635
Coming home in uniform and walking back

314
00:10:07,635 --> 00:10:10,109
through the same factory gate was a

315
00:10:10,109 --> 00:10:12,372
moment a lot of veterans of that

316
00:10:12,372 --> 00:10:13,574
era never forgot.

317
00:10:13,923 --> 00:10:15,084
It wasn't perfect.

318
00:10:15,264 --> 00:10:17,038
The work could be dull,

319
00:10:17,038 --> 00:10:18,504
hard and dangerous.

320
00:10:18,684 --> 00:10:21,050
Women were often paid far less than

321
00:10:21,050 --> 00:10:22,469
men for the same job,

322
00:10:22,469 --> 00:10:24,633
and many people were shut out of

323
00:10:24,633 --> 00:10:27,269
good jobs entirely because of the color

324
00:10:27,269 --> 00:10:28,215
of their skin.

325
00:10:28,395 --> 00:10:30,419
But for millions of families,

326
00:10:30,419 --> 00:10:32,302
a steady job meant a house,

327
00:10:32,302 --> 00:10:34,884
a car, and children who might someday

328
00:10:34,884 --> 00:10:35,861
go to college.

329
00:10:36,210 --> 00:10:38,112
That first job was the moment you

330
00:10:38,112 --> 00:10:39,380
stopped being a child.

331
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:41,766
Nobody gave you a certificate for it.

332
00:10:41,946 --> 00:10:43,339
There was no ceremony.

333
00:10:43,519 --> 00:10:45,519
But you came home one Friday with

334
00:10:45,519 --> 00:10:47,337
your own money in your pocket,

335
00:10:47,337 --> 00:10:49,216
and your father looked at you a

336
00:10:49,216 --> 00:10:51,580
little differently at the dinner table,

337
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:53,701
and you knew something had changed.

338
00:10:54,050 --> 00:10:56,405
Maybe you still have a pay stub

339
00:10:56,405 --> 00:10:59,139
somewhere, or an old employee badge,

340
00:10:59,139 --> 00:11:01,798
or a photograph of yourself in that

341
00:11:01,798 --> 00:11:04,836
uniform, standing a little too straight.

342
00:11:05,016 --> 00:11:05,693
If you do,

343
00:11:05,693 --> 00:11:07,048
take it out tonight.

344
00:11:07,228 --> 00:11:08,668
Look at that young face.

345
00:11:08,848 --> 00:11:11,039
That person had no idea what was

346
00:11:11,039 --> 00:11:11,518
coming.

347
00:11:11,698 --> 00:11:13,233
But they were ready to work for

348
00:11:13,233 --> 00:11:13,381
it.

349
00:11:13,731 --> 00:11:15,554
So tell us in the comments.

350
00:11:15,734 --> 00:11:17,653
What was your very first job,

351
00:11:17,653 --> 00:11:19,240
and how much did it pay?

352
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:21,046
We read every one of them.

353
00:11:21,226 --> 00:11:22,265
And stay with us,

354
00:11:22,265 --> 00:11:24,405
because next we're going to look at

355
00:11:24,405 --> 00:11:26,728
what a whole paycheck really bought in

356
00:11:26,728 --> 00:11:27,890
nineteen sixty-two.

357
00:11:28,070 --> 00:11:29,649
Thank you for spending this time with

358
00:11:29,649 --> 00:11:29,777
me.
